Project Name: Deploy MongoDB Express AWS


# MongoDB + Mongo Express on AWS EKS with Helm, EBS, NGINX Ingress, and AWS Load Balancer

## Project Overview

This demo project deploys MongoDB and Mongo Express on an AWS EKS Kubernetes cluster.

The project is based on a previous Helm demo where MongoDB was installed using the Bitnami Helm chart, Mongo Express was deployed with custom Kubernetes YAML, and NGINX Ingress was used to expose the application externally. In this version, the infrastructure runs on AWS EKS instead of a local or Linode Kubernetes cluster.

The goal of this project is to practice:

- Creating and managing an AWS EKS cluster
- Using Helm to deploy MongoDB
- Overriding default Helm chart values with a custom values file
- Using AWS EBS for persistent MongoDB storage
- Deploying Mongo Express with custom Kubernetes manifests
- Exposing Mongo Express through NGINX Ingress
- Automatically creating an AWS Load Balancer through a Kubernetes `LoadBalancer` service

## Architecture

```text
Internet
   ↓
AWS Network Load Balancer
   ↓
NGINX Ingress Controller
   ↓
Mongo Express Ingress
   ↓
Mongo Express Service
   ↓
Mongo Express Pod
   ↓
MongoDB Service
   ↓
MongoDB Pod
   ↓
AWS EBS Persistent Volume
```

## Technologies Used

- AWS EKS
- AWS EC2 worker nodes
- AWS EBS
- AWS Load Balancer
- Kubernetes
- Helm
- Bitnami MongoDB Helm chart
- Mongo Express
- NGINX Ingress Controller
- kubectl
- eksctl
- AWS CLI

## Kubernetes Namespace

All application resources are deployed into the namespace:

```bash
mongo-demo
```

## MongoDB Deployment

MongoDB is deployed using the Bitnami Helm chart:

```bash
helm install mongodb bitnami/mongodb -n mongo-demo -f helm-mongodb.yaml
```

For this demo, MongoDB was deployed in standalone mode because the test cluster used small AWS instances.

Example `helm-mongodb.yaml`:

```yaml
architecture: standalone

auth:
  enabled: true
  rootUser: root
  rootPassword: "<strong-password>"

persistence:
  enabled: true
  storageClass: ebs-gp3
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  size: 8Gi

service:
  type: ClusterIP

resources:
  requests:
    cpu: 100m
    memory: 256Mi
  limits:
    cpu: 500m
    memory: 512Mi
```

MongoDB is kept internal inside the Kubernetes cluster and is not exposed directly to the internet.

## Persistent Storage

AWS EBS is used for MongoDB persistent storage.

A custom StorageClass was created:

```yaml
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: ebs-gp3
provisioner: ebs.csi.aws.com
parameters:
  type: gp3
  fsType: ext4
reclaimPolicy: Retain
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
allowVolumeExpansion: true
```

The EBS CSI driver is required so Kubernetes can dynamically provision EBS volumes for PersistentVolumeClaims.

The StorageClass uses:

```yaml
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
```

This means the EBS volume is created only after Kubernetes knows which node the MongoDB pod will run on.

## Mongo Express Deployment

Mongo Express is deployed using a custom Kubernetes YAML file.

It connects to MongoDB using the internal Kubernetes DNS name:

```text
mongodb.mongo-demo.svc.cluster.local
```

Mongo Express is exposed internally by a ClusterIP service:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: mongo-express-service
  namespace: mongo-demo
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  selector:
    app: mongo-express
  ports:
    - name: http
      port: 8081
      targetPort: 8081
```

A Kubernetes Secret is used to store the MongoDB connection string for Mongo Express.

Example:

```bash
kubectl create secret generic mongo-express-url \
  -n mongo-demo \
  --from-literal=mongodb-url='mongodb://root:<encoded-password>@mongodb.mongo-demo.svc.cluster.local:27017/admin?authSource=admin'
```

## NGINX Ingress and AWS Load Balancer

NGINX Ingress Controller is installed using Helm.

The ingress controller service is configured as a Kubernetes `LoadBalancer` service:

```yaml
controller:
  replicaCount: 1

  service:
    type: LoadBalancer
    annotations:
      service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-type: "nlb"
      service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-scheme: "internet-facing"
      service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-cross-zone-load-balancing-enabled: "true"

  resources:
    requests:
      cpu: 100m
      memory: 128Mi
    limits:
      cpu: 300m
      memory: 384Mi
```

This causes AWS to automatically create an external Network Load Balancer.

Install command:

```bash
helm install ingress-nginx ingress-nginx/ingress-nginx \
  -n ingress-nginx \
  --create-namespace \
  -f ingress-nginx-values.yaml
```

## Mongo Express Ingress

Mongo Express is exposed through an Ingress resource:

```yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: mongo-express
  namespace: mongo-demo
spec:
  ingressClassName: nginx
  rules:
    - http:
        paths:
          - path: /
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: mongo-express-service
                port:
                  number: 8081
```

For testing, no custom hostname is required.

The AWS Load Balancer DNS name can be used directly:

```text
http://<AWS-LOAD-BALANCER-DNS-NAME>
```

## Important Lessons Learned

### 1. `t3.micro` was too small for this setup

A single `t3.micro` node was not enough because EKS system pods already consumed most available pod capacity.

System components included:

- `aws-node`
- `kube-proxy`
- `coredns`
- `ebs-csi-controller`
- `ebs-csi-node`
- `metrics-server`

The MongoDB pod stayed in `Pending` state with this error:

```text
Too many pods
```

The solution was to use a larger or more suitable worker node, such as `t3.small`.

### 2. PVC stayed pending because the pod could not be scheduled

The MongoDB PVC showed:

```text
WaitForFirstConsumer
```

This was expected because the StorageClass used:

```yaml
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
```

The EBS volume was not created until Kubernetes could schedule the MongoDB pod to a node.

### 3. Mongo Express password issue

Mongo Express failed because the MongoDB root password contained a special character:

```text
/
```

In a MongoDB connection string, special characters must be URL-encoded.

Example:

```text
/  →  %2F
```

The fix was to create a connection string secret with the encoded password.

## Useful Commands

Check all pods:

```bash
kubectl get pods -A
```

Check MongoDB and Mongo Express resources:

```bash
kubectl get pods -n mongo-demo
kubectl get pvc -n mongo-demo
kubectl get svc -n mongo-demo
```

Check secrets:

```bash
kubectl get secret -n mongo-demo
```

Decode MongoDB root password:

```bash
kubectl get secret mongodb \
  -n mongo-demo \
  -o jsonpath="{.data.mongodb-root-password}" | base64 -d
echo
```

Decode Mongo Express MongoDB URL:

```bash
kubectl get secret mongo-express-url \
  -n mongo-demo \
  -o jsonpath="{.data.mongodb-url}" | base64 -d
echo
```

Check NGINX Ingress Load Balancer:

```bash
kubectl get svc -n ingress-nginx
```

Get AWS Load Balancer hostname:

```bash
kubectl get svc ingress-nginx-controller \
  -n ingress-nginx \
  -o jsonpath="{.status.loadBalancer.ingress[0].hostname}"
echo
```

Check Mongo Express logs:

```bash
kubectl logs deploy/mongo-express -n mongo-demo
```

Check NGINX Ingress logs:

```bash
kubectl logs deploy/ingress-nginx-controller -n ingress-nginx
```

## Cleanup

To avoid AWS costs, delete the resources after testing:

```bash
helm uninstall ingress-nginx -n ingress-nginx
kubectl delete namespace ingress-nginx

kubectl delete -f mongo-express.yaml
helm uninstall mongodb -n mongo-demo
kubectl delete pvc --all -n mongo-demo
kubectl delete namespace mongo-demo

eksctl delete cluster \
  --name mongo-express-cluster \
  --region eu-central-1
```

Also check for leftover AWS resources:

- EKS cluster
- EC2 worker nodes
- EBS volumes
- EBS snapshots
- AWS Load Balancers
- Target Groups
- CloudFormation stacks
- S3 buckets
- IAM roles created for the demo

## Final Result

This project demonstrates a working AWS EKS-based deployment pattern for MongoDB and Mongo Express using Helm, persistent storage, NGINX Ingress, and an AWS Network Load Balancer.
